# V. Risk factors

#1. Periodicity of the mortgage market
Equifax relies more on the US mortgage market than TransUnion and Experian. Mortgage revenue is highly sensitive to the interest rate environment, and rising interest rates directly discourage refinancing and new lending activities, affecting certification services and credit reporting revenue.

##2. Data security and reputational risks
The large-scale data leak in 2017 affected about 147 million US consumers, resulting in more than $1 billion in fines and settlement costs paid by companies. Although the company has since significantly increased its security input, any new data leak could have caused serious financial and reputational damage.

##3. Cloud transition implementation risks
Companies have invested significant resources in cloud transformation, but business interruptions, less than expected efficiency gains or system availability in the transition process may affect client trust and financial performance.

##4. Regulatory policy changes
The same regulatory risks as TransUnion: regulators like CFPB are increasingly concerned about credit bureau pricing and competition. Rapid increases in credit reporting costs may trigger stricter price regulation.

#5. Slow down the recruitment market
Employers’ services are directly linked to labour market activity.

